\par\par \b \uc2 FAA\uc2  Launches Review of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  --- Company employees report pressure from the aerospace giant over safety issues\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Tangel \par \uc2 707 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  is launching a broad review of how \uc2 Boeing Co\uc2 . employees handle safety matters on the agency's behalf after some company engineers said they face undue pressure, according to an agency letter and people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 FAA\uc2  survey conducted this year found 35% of a small sample of certain \uc2 Boeing\uc2  employees reported problems including pressure and hurdles to transparency, according to an Aug. 19 agency letter to \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . Some surveyed employees, who are part of a group empowered by the agency to assist its work, said they encountered difficulties in being transparent with regulators, according to the letter, which was viewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 U.S. aviation regulators have long relied on aerospace-company employees to act on their behalf for performing certain tasks, such as signing off on routine safety assessments or approving aircraft for delivery. The problems cited by \uc2 Boeing\uc2  employees in the survey "indicate the environment does not support independence" of those who are empowered to act on the agency's behalf, according to the letter, which was signed by Ian Won, acting manager of the FAA's \uc2 Boeing\uc2  oversight office in the Seattle area.\par \par \uc2 A Boeing spokeswoman said the company takes "these matters with the utmost seriousness" and is working to bolster the independence of its employees who work on the \uc2 FAA\uc2 's behalf.\par \par \uc2 "We have consistently reinforced with our team that delegated authority is a privilege and that we must work every day to be trusted with the responsibility," she said. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has directed that its \uc2 FAA\uc2  delegates "must be accorded the same respect and deference that is shown to our regulator."\par \par \uc2 The Chicago-based aerospace giant has faced setbacks in recent years related to engineering and quality issues with various commercial, military and space programs.\par \par \uc2 Problems cited by the August letter are similar to ones identified by a 2016 internal \uc2 Boeing\uc2  survey, which was highlighted by a congressional investigation into two fatal crashes of the plane maker's 737 MAX jets in late 2018 and early 2019. The crashes, which took 346 lives, led \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to revamp how it handles engineering matters and prompted U.S. lawmakers to require changes to how regulators certify aircraft are safe.\par \par \uc2 The recent \uc2 FAA\uc2  survey was conducted from May 2021 through July 2021, according to the letter. It involved 32 employees out of approximately 1,400 \uc2 Boeing\uc2  staff who work on the \uc2 FAA\uc2 's behalf, according to people familiar with the matter. The letter said the "concerns require an objective review and further fact finding," potentially including an anonymous, independent survey of all members of what is formally known as Boeing's Organization Designation Authorization unit. The Boeing spokeswoman said the company was working with the \uc2 FAA\uc2  to follow its guidance.\par \par \uc2 The two-page letter came with excerpts from interviews with \uc2 Boeing\uc2  employees. The employees, who were quoted anonymously, told the \uc2 FAA\uc2  that the pressure they felt wasn't necessarily overt and could also come from the engineering ranks pushing to stay on schedule.\par \par \uc2 "I feel undue pressure but I stand up to it," one \uc2 Boeing\uc2  employee was quoted by the letter as saying.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  letter said that "\uc2 Boeing\uc2 's company culture appears to hamper" its FAA-empowered employees "from communicating openly with the \uc2 FAA\uc2 ." The letter cited one \uc2 Boeing\uc2  employee who told the agency: "I am very aware that my bringing up issues is not appreciated."\par \par \uc2 The increased scrutiny comes as the \uc2 FAA\uc2  prepares to study the company's culture, as required by a new federal law requiring changes to how the U.S. certifies airplanes for commercial service. Those changes came in the wake of the MAX crashes, and after U.S. lawmakers criticized the \uc2 FAA\uc2  and its oversight of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  throughout the aircraft's development.\par \par \uc2 Before the legislation became law, an investigation by the U.S. House Transportation Committee turned up the 2016 internal \uc2 Boeing\uc2  survey that showed roughly one in three employees who responded felt "potential undue pressure" from managers across various commercial planes.\par \par \uc2 The Boeing spokeswoman said the company has worked hard to "build a culture of open communication, trust, transparency and respect."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210824eh8o0001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
