\par\par \b \uc2 State Street Moves Out of New York Offices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dawn Lim \par \uc2 850 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Financial giant \uc2 State Street Corp\uc2 . is vacating its two New York City office locations.\par \par \uc2 Executives at the Boston firm told New York staffers they won't be returning to its Midtown Manhattan offices, according to people familiar with the matter. It expects to sublease the two offices near Rockefeller Center to other companies.\par \par \uc2 Many of \uc2 State Street\uc2 's New York employees have worked remotely since the pandemic's early days. The financial firm, like others, sent staffers to work from home when infections started surging across the country. Later, U.S. employees who needed an office could go into work if they followed safety protocols. The firm's New York offices remained sparsely occupied.\par \par \uc2 In May, \uc2 State Street\uc2  told New York staffers to prepare for the closure of their offices. More than 500 employees across the firm's custody bank and money-management businesses will be affected, some of the people said.\par \par \uc2 State Street\uc2  is giving New York-based employees the option to work in other offices in New Jersey and Stamford, Conn. But the firm isn't directing New York-based staffers to spend time in those offices, letting different groups across \uc2 State Street\uc2  determine the mix of in-person and remote work that suits them. \uc2 State Street\uc2  also secured some co-working space in Manhattan for those who want to use it.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the firm said \uc2 State Street\uc2  changed its real-estate strategy in New York to accommodate a hybrid workforce.\par \par \uc2 "We have taken a diligent look at our real-estate footprint in NYC," spokesman Edward Patterson said. He added that the firm is "ensuring that our realty needs are in line with where our employees will be working."\par \par \uc2 State Street\uc2 's pivot is part of a broader re-evaluation of New York City offices by financial firms.\par \par \uc2 Firms across business lines are questioning whether New York City's costs, taxes and uncertain recovery from the pandemic make it worthwhile to maintain the same Manhattan office presence as before. In finance, some banks and other firms have ordered workers back to the office, while others have been slower to return to New York's towers.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc\uc2 ., while maintaining its New York office, is planning to expand the head count in West Palm Beach, Fla., by as much as several hundred in the near future, said a person familiar with the matter. \uc2 Paul Singer\uc2 's hedge fund Elliott Investment Management LP moved its headquarters from New York to Florida at the start of 2021.\par \par \uc2 At stake is the office culture of financial firms and vitality of the nation's financial capital.\par \par \uc2 "It's certainly a wake-up call that we cannot take for granted New York's position as the financial capital," said \uc2 Kathryn Wylde\uc2 , chief executive of business group Partnership for New York City. "During the pandemic, people have learned they can work profitably from anywhere -- and that's a competitive threat to the concentration of talent in New York."\par \par \uc2 Adding to the New York dilemma, the spread of the Delta variant of the coronavirus has created logistical headaches for firms that need to make employees feel comfortable commuting and working in close quarters. Asset-management giant \uc2 BlackRock Inc\uc2 . recently delayed the formal date workers have to return to its New York headquarters by about a month, to October.\par \par \uc2 Even before the pandemic, some asset-management firms had been moving into smaller, lower-wage cities. Intense competition over the past decade forced many to take steps to reduce costs and protect profit margins.\par \par \uc2 AllianceBernstein Holding LP\uc2  in 2018 decided to shift its headquarters from New York to Nashville, Tenn. The money manager cited lower state, city and property taxes as among reasons for the move. It plans to relocate 1,250 jobs to Nashville by 2024, and is 70% of the way toward that goal, a spokeswoman said. It will officially open its new Nashville office this fall.\par \par \uc2 State Street\uc2 's most well-known presence in New York is the "Fearless Girl" statue, a fixture outside the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  on Wall Street. \uc2 State Street\uc2 's exchange-traded funds are used by traders all around Wall Street, and the firm provides custody services forNew York investment firmsamong others.\par \par \uc2 But a major chunk of its U.S. staff works out of the Boston area.\par \par \uc2 Subleasing NewYork offices will help generate additional revenue for \uc2 State Street\uc2 , without the firm having to pay costs of breaking those leases.In the past year, low interest rates have eaten into custody banks' bottom line. This year, the firm announced plans to cut 1,200 jobs, or 3% of its workforce, and to reduce office space for cost savings.\par \par \uc2 The State Street spokesman said there will beno changes to how it services clients in New York City and the surrounding area. He said the firm is committed to clients, business lines, corporate functions and staffers in the greater New York area, who include members of its executive leadership.\par \par \uc2 The "Fearless Girl" statue will remain on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210817eh8h00018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
