\par\par \b \uc2 World News: Canada Border Agents End Slowdown\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Vieira \par \uc2 506 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA -- Canada's border agents ended a nearly daylong partial strike Friday after their union and the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  struck a new labor deal, removing a potential roadblock for Americans, who can begin visiting Monday for the first time in 16 months.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Public Service Alliance of Canada\uc2 , which negotiates on behalf of about 9,000 employees at the \uc2 Canada Border Services Agency\uc2 , said it clinched a four-year agreement after more than 36 hours of mediator-supervised talks. A mediator was brought in midweek to help resolve differences, which the union said focused on working conditions and compensation.\par \par \uc2 Government and union negotiators failed to reach a deal before a 6 a.m. ET Friday deadline, and that prompted unionized customs and immigrations officers to slow down the processing of trucks and travelers, and refrain from activity that isn't required under the job description. The move caused long delays for shippers and travelers trying to cross the land border into Canada.\par \par \uc2 "We are relieved that CBSA and the government finally stepped up to address the most important issues for our members to avoid a prolonged labor dispute," said \uc2 Chris Aylward\uc2 , national president of the Public Service Alliance. He added the work-to-rule campaign would cease immediately.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  said the tentative agreement provides pay increases of 2%-plus in the first two years, along with other enhanced benefits, such as extended parental leave. Border agents have been without a contract for more than three years.\par \par \uc2 The labor disruption had an immediate impact. Video posted Friday to social media showed a lengthy line of trucks stuck on Interstate 75 in Detroit, en route to the Ambassador Bridge which connects Michigan to Canada. The \uc2 U.S. Department of Transportation\uc2  said the bridge is the single busiest international land-border crossing in North America, accounting for 27% of the roughly $400 billion in annual trade between the U.S. and Canada.\par \par \uc2 Data from the Canadian border authority's website indicated commercial truckers in the U.S. faced delays of two to four hours to enter Canada at major land crossings in Michigan, Vermont, New York, North Dakota, Montana, and Washington.\par \par \uc2 A prolonged strike would have affected Canadian manufacturers' "ability to get the essential components and goods to sustain global supply chains and threatens thousands of Canadian businesses," said Dennis Darby, head of the \uc2 Canadian Manufacturers and Exporters\uc2 , a lobby group.\par \par \uc2 For Canadian businesses in the hospitality and hotel sector, the big concern was any labor disruption would dissuade American tourists from visiting Canada starting Monday, when the country begins to lift pandemic-related border restrictions. Canada is opening its borders to fully vaccinated U.S. citizens living in the U.S. to enter for tourism and recreation purposes, subject to obtaining a negative molecular Covid-19 test 72 hours before arrival.\par \par \uc2 Canadian businesses tied to tourism and hospitality are counting on a partial reopening at border to help salvage what is left of the summer season.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210807eh870004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
