\par\par \b \uc2 World News: Canadian Strike Threat Looms Over Border Reopening\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Vieira \par \uc2 357 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA -- Canada's plan to reopen its border next week to U.S. tourists could be in trouble as the union representing customs and immigration officers said it is ready to cut services at land crossings, airports and shipping ports Friday unless a new labor pact is struck.\par \par \uc2 A labor disruption could upend the North American economy, given the tight supply-chain links between the U.S. and Canada. Moreover, it could deal another blow to Canadian businesses in the travel, tourism and hospitality sectors.\par \par \uc2 Canada said last month it would reopen its borders to fully vaccinated U.S. citizens and permanent residents living in the U.S. to enter for tourism and recreation purposes, starting Monday.\par \par \uc2 "A strike could dissuade fully vaccinated Americans from making a trip if they are concerned about undue delays," said \uc2 Mark Agnew\uc2 , vice president of policy at Canadian Chamber of Commerce.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Department of Homeland Security\uc2  has extended its land-border restrictions with Canada and Mexico through Aug. 21.\par \par \uc2 Public Service Alliance of Canada\uc2 , which is handling negotiations for the roughly 9,000 employees at the \uc2 Canada Border Services Agency\uc2 , said it served formal strike notice Tuesday to the federal government, informing it labor disruptions could start at 6 a.m. Friday unless they reach a new collective-bargaining deal. Employees have been without a contract for about three years.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two-thirds of the unionized border agents are classified as essential, and can't strike. Instead, the union said these workers would conduct a work-to-rule campaign, which it warns likely would cause a decline in productivity and "long and unavoidable delays at Canada's borders."\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  spokeswoman said Ottawa requested a mediator's help to resolve outstanding issues. A union spokesman said the government also asked that the two sides resume negotiations, and the union agreed. However, he added, "it's unlikely a deal will be reached before Friday." The union said the main sticking points are improved working conditions and better pay and benefits that align with other law-enforcement authorities.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210805eh850001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
