\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Activision Employees Stage Walkouts --- Demonstrators call for changes in workplace culture amid suit alleging gender bias\b0\par\par\uc2 By Meghan Bobrowsky and Sarah E. Needleman \par \uc2 518 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Current and former \uc2 Activision Blizzard Inc\uc2 . employees participated in virtual and in-person walkouts Wednesday to call for changes to the company's workplace culture amid disappointment with its reaction to allegations made in a recent lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The suit, which was filed by \uc2 California's Department of Fair Employment and Housing\uc2  last week, accuses the largest U.S. videogame publisher by market value of paying female employees less than their male counterparts and providing them with fewer opportunities to advance. It also alleges that \uc2 Activision\uc2  ignored complaints by female employees of blatant harassment, discrimination and retaliation.\par \par \uc2 Activision\uc2  previously said it would fight the charges and that the lawsuit included distorted, and in many cases false, descriptions of its past. Late Tuesday, hours before the planned walkouts, Chief Executive \uc2 Bobby Kotick\uc2  said the company hired a law firm to investigate complaints of sexual harassment and gender-pay discrimination.\par \par \uc2 "Our initial responses to the issues we face together, and to your concerns, were, quite frankly, tone deaf," Mr. Kotick said in a Tuesday night statement. "It is imperative that we acknowledge all perspectives and experiences and respect the feelings of those who have been mistreated in any way. I am sorry that we did not provide the right empathy and understanding."\par \par \uc2 Those steps didn't go far enough, some employees say, explaining that it didn't address the need for greater pay transparency to ensure equality or employee selection of a third party to audit human resources among other things.\par \par \uc2 More than 3,000 current and former \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2  workers signed a letter sent to management earlier in the week calling the company's lawsuit response as "abhorrent and insulting."\par \par \uc2 The in-person event was held outside of the Blizzard campus in Irvine, Calif. Across the gaming community, in solidarity with the campaign, people used the hashtag #ActiBlizzWalkout in social-media posts.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the walkout, a representative for Activision said the company is committed to fostering a safe, inclusive and rewarding environment for all its employees and that those who participate won't be penalized or denied pay.\par \par \uc2 Former Blizzard software engineer Cher Scarlett said she participated in the virtual walkout by replacing her \uc2 Twitter\uc2  profile photos with a plain blue square to bring awareness to the lawsuit and gender bias in gaming. Ms. Scarlett, a 36-year-old Seattle resident, also said she donated $100 to a nonprofit that supports women in the gaming industry.\par \par \uc2 Current and former \uc2 Ubisoft Entertainment SA\uc2  employees signed a separate letter supporting \uc2 Activision\uc2  workers, citing previous harassment, discrimination and bullying allegations at their own company.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Paris-based \uc2 Ubisoft\uc2 , which makes "Assassin's Creed" among other videogames, was accused of institutional sexual harassment in a suit filed in a criminal court in France on behalf of a game-workers union and several employees. A spokeswoman for Ubisoft declined to comment on the suit but said the company doesn't and won't tolerate abuse, harassment or discrimination.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210729eh7t0001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
