\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Volvo to Resume U.S. Truck Production Amid Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Smith \par \uc2 361 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Volvo Trucks North America\uc2  says it plans to resume production at its truck manufacturing plant in Dublin, Va., despite a third vote by unionized workers rejecting a tentative contract settlement aimed at ending a monthlong walkout at the factory.\par \par \uc2 The division of Swedish vehicle maker \uc2 Volvo AB\uc2  said it was taking the action after hitting an impasse with negotiators for the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  over new attempts to reach a contract settlement that the plant's roughly 2,900 UAW-represented workers would accept.\par \par \uc2 "We need to safeguard our future, and start building trucks for the many customers and dealers whose businesses and livelihoods depend on our products," Franky Marchand, vice president and general manager of the plant, said in a statement Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The company said it would implement the terms of the contract that management and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  negotiators agreed to on July 1, the third agreement that negotiators have reached that rank-and-file workers have rejected in recent months.\par \par \uc2 The company said any employee who returned to work on Monday or after would receive the wage increases and benefits outlined in the July 1 agreement, except for ratification bonuses.\par \par \uc2 A UAW spokesman said the strike is continuing, and that the union is evaluating Volvo's position and the union's legal options. "A new vote is scheduled Wednesday for the bargaining unit members on the company's last, best and final offer," he said.\par \par \uc2 Volvo said the strike, the second the workers have staged this year over the contract terms, is hurting its customers and has delayed a project to expand and upgrade the facility. The factory produces all Class 8 heavy-duty Volvo trucks sold in North America. A spokesman declined to comment on the financial impact of the strike.\par \par \uc2 The standoff comes as Volvo and other equipment makers are also coping with shortages of semiconductors amid broader upheaval in global supply chains since the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Marchand said the company's last offer "delivered significant wage gains and first-class benefits for our employees, and 40% of \uc2 UAW\uc2  voters supported it."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210713eh7d0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
