\par\par \b \uc2 CFO Journal: Companies Eat Costs to Shrink Offices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kristin Broughton and Nina Trentmann \par \uc2 1,050 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Companies expect to reap millions of dollars in savings in the years ahead as they scale back on office space after the coronavirus pandemic emptied workplaces around the country. However, some are paying in the short term for their decision to retrench.\par \par \uc2 Online listings company \uc2 Yelp Inc\uc2 ., consumer loan provider \uc2 Affirm Holdings Inc\uc2 . and drug distributor \uc2 McKesson Corp\uc2 . in recent weeks have disclosed one-time charges related to plans to shrink their real-estate footprint. They are among many businesses that are subletting office space, choosing not to renew leases or taking other steps to slim down after giving employees more flexibility to work from home.\par \par \uc2 Finance chiefs have spent months weighing the costs and benefits associated with getting rid of unused office space as businesses consider whether to return to the office. While companies such as \uc2 Facebook Inc\uc2 . are allowing some employees to work from home permanently, others, including \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2 ., are asking most of them to spend a few days a week in the office. A third group of businesses -- among them \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc\uc2 . -- is asking employees to come back to the office full time.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco-based Affirm recently announced its roughly 1,340 employees would be given a choice between working from home indefinitely and returning to the office. "Covid changed everything, and we became a remote-first company," said \uc2 Michael Linford\uc2 , the company's chief financial officer.\par \par \uc2 The company decided to sublease one of its two offices in San Francisco to recover some of its rent, as it doesn't expect all employees to come back. Affirm booked a roughly $11 million charge during the quarter ended March 31 for subleasing the office, which it has rented until 2026. It continues to operate offices in New York, Chicago, Toronto, Pittsburgh and Salt Lake City.\par \par \uc2 Affirm booked $400.1 million in operating expenses, which include rent, during the quarter ended March 31, up from $219.8 million a year earlier, due in part to higher technology costs and stock-based compensation associated with the company's initial public offering in January.\par \par \uc2 Companies book impairment charges when they sublease office space for less money than what they pay for it, according to Jonathan Milian, an accounting professor at Florida International University. The charge represents a decline in the asset's value, he said.\par \par \uc2 More than a year into the pandemic, demand for office space has decreased significantly. During the first quarter, the amount of U.S. office space that became vacant exceeded the amount of space that was leased by 34.8 million square feet, commercial real-estate services and investment firm \uc2 CBRE Group Inc\uc2 . said. The difference between the two figures -- known in the industry as net absorption -- was the largest in at least two decades, CBRE said.\par \par \uc2 About 16.4% of office space across the country stood empty at the end of the first quarter, up from 13% a year earlier, according to \uc2 Cushman & Wakefield PLC\uc2 , a commercial real-estate firm.\par \par \uc2 As with Affirm, San Francisco-based \uc2 Yelp\uc2  earlier this year told its roughly 3,900 employees that they could continue working from home permanently or come into the office. "We've found that our employees are generally even more effective when they can choose where they want to live and work," CFO \uc2 David Schwarzbach\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Over the past two months, \uc2 Yelp\uc2  has signed sublease agreements for some of its office space in New York and San Francisco. The company expects to book an $11 million impairment charge in the second quarter in connection with the agreements. Over time, however, \uc2 Yelp\uc2  expects to save an estimated $10 million to $12 million a year through 2024.\par \par \uc2 Yelp\uc2 's total costs during the first quarter were $240.7 million, down 13% from a year earlier. The company reported a net loss of $5.8 million, compared with a $15.5 million loss in the year-ago period.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 McKesson\uc2  in May said it plans to adopt a hybrid work model. The Irving, Texas-based company, which has about 76,000 employees around the world, is shrinking its real-estate footprint and expects to book between $180 million to $280 million in related restructuring charges over the next year, CFO \uc2 Britt Vitalone\uc2  said in May.\par \par \uc2 The reductions in office space, once fully implemented, are expected to save \uc2 McKesson\uc2  between $60 million and $80 million a year, Mr. Vitalone said. The company posted a $4.5 billion loss for the fiscal year ended March 31, compared with a $900 million profit a year earlier. It declined to comment further.\par \par \uc2 Occupancy rates for offices have increased in recent months, although they remain well below pre-pandemic levels. During the week ended June 23, the average occupancy rate in 10 major U.S. metro areas was 32%, up from 22% a year earlier, according to \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2 , which operates security systems in approximately 2,600 U.S. office buildings. Kastle tracks how many people are visiting buildings based on anonymized data from its swipe-entry systems.\par \par \uc2 "The fact that the pandemic lasted so long made people appreciate what they are missing when they are not in the office," said \uc2 David O'Reilly\uc2 , chief executive at \uc2 Howard Hughes Corp\uc2 ., which owns, builds and operates commercial, mixed-use and residential real estate.\par \par \uc2 Occupancy rates vary widely between cities. Only about 20% of offices in New York and San Francisco were being used during the week ended June 23, while cities in Texas had occupancy rates of around 50%, \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2  said. Technology companies such as \uc2 HP Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Oracle Corp\uc2 . said they plan to move their headquarters from high-cost cities in California to Texas.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco-based \uc2 Salesforce.com Inc\uc2 . is another company that is currently evaluating how much real estate it will need in the months and years ahead -- and whether it can save money by scaling back. The software provider has surveyed its employees over the past year and is offering the choice to either work from home or in the office, CFO \uc2 Amy Weaver\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "The number-one thing we heard from employees is that flexibility is important to them," Ms. Weaver said. \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  employed 56,606 people at the end of January.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210706eh760000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
