\par\par \b \uc2 Teamsters Pledge Aid In Organizing Union For Amazon Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 767 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 One of America's largest labor unions passed a resolution Thursday designed to aid \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . workers in eventually achieving a union contract, a move aimed at putting further labor pressure on the e-commerce giant following a recent failed unionization effort in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Representatives from the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  drafted the resolution after recent years in which \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has become a central focus for the group as the company's impact on blue-collar workers has increased.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 99% of more than 1,500 delegates from the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , which represents over one million employees across North America including package delivery, warehouse and trucking workers, voted in favor of the resolution during the union's 30th annual convention hosted online. The resolution, titled "Building Worker Power at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 ," stipulates that the union would "supply all resources necessary" and eventually create a special division to organize workers at the company.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Amazon\uc2  is changing the nature of work in our country and touches many core Teamster industries and employers," the union said in the resolution. "\uc2 Teamsters\uc2  across North America have been and will continue to stand in solidarity with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in their effort to find a true voice on the job."\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  didn't respond to a request for comment on the resolution. In the past, the company has said that a union is unnecessary because its workers already receive the pay and benefits that a union would bargain for. It has also said it works hard to listen to its workers and make improvements.\par \par \uc2 The effort by \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees to organize sustained a large blow in April when workers at a warehouse in Bessemer, Ala., overwhelmingly voted down joining the Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union. The closely followed vote was seen as a barometer for labor unions in the U.S. The RWDSU has appealed the election results.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  haven't said how much money or staff it will devote to the matter.\par \par \uc2 The group doesn't represent \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers but has been increasingly devoting resources to organize the company's employees. While the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have passed resolutions previously focused on industries or companies, this is the first targeting \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . The group hosts a convention every five years to outline priorities until the next convention. \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2 , which helps deliver a percentage of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  packages, represents the single largest employer in the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It creates a broad approach and a good message and activates our best resource, which is our membership," Randy Korgan, director of Amazon at the Teamsters, said of the resolution.\par \par \uc2 Since the beginning of the pandemic, workers have communicated with the union more frequently and sought guidance about ways to organize, including with strikes, union officials have said. Union officials have sought various ways to organize employees -- such as with strikes -- aside from gaining representation through the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , which many times handles official unionization efforts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  appointed Mr. Korgan as national director of Amazon last year to focus on the company. The union said it has maintained consistent membership, with fluctuations during downward economic times. The group said it is tied as the fifth-largest labor union in terms of membership in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  resolution outlines \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's growing power in retail and other industries. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has seen sustained demand during the pandemic as customers flocked to its site for supplies. The company, which also runs wildly successful cloud computing and advertising businesses, made $386 billion last year as its market value nearly doubled.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  continues to face pressure from its workforce and outside voices over the treatment of its hourly warehouse workers and delivery drivers, many of whom are contracted by third-party firms. Critics have often focused on the rate at which workers are expected to do their duties, with employees often having to package or sort hundreds of items per hour with few breaks.\par \par \uc2 The company has long had high turnover at its facilities, and its injury rates have been higher than the national average for its industry. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has said it gives workers reasonable quotas and that it monitors workplace injuries more closely than other companies, potentially inflating numbers.\par \par \uc2 While the resolution serves to unite the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and allies against \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , the union faces ample challenges. No \uc2 Amazon\uc2  worker is represented by a union, and organizing workers has been difficult. That is in part because U.S. labor laws can largely favor employers, according to labor researchers, for example by hosting antiunion meetings at facilities.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210625eh6p0002e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
