\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 Work Flexibility Is Hardly a Holy Grail --- Many employees aren't eager to go back to the office but companies have more to consider than just their feelings\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Forman \par \uc2 795 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 Many of today's workers have landed the upper hand when it comes to where and when they work. But no one should get too comfortable.\par \par \uc2 As demand roars back across the U.S. economy and workers are flush with pandemic savings, the little guy is suddenly wielding more power. Zillow, for example, has said it is allowing most employees to permanently work remotely, betting such a policy will yield the best talent.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  also said this month that all full-time employees who can do their jobs remotely would be able to do so more permanently with permission, in some cases even across international borders. \uc2 Shopify\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Dropbox\uc2 , \uc2 Instacart\uc2  and Slack, among others, have announced permanent remote policies for many of their employees. \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  and \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  recently terminated sizable San Francisco leases, paying $113 million and nearly $90 million, respectively, to do so.\par \par \uc2 Employees definitely want more flexibility. In a survey across 30,000 Americans, 30% said they never want to return to the office, according to data from the Working From Home Research Project. The trendy notion of the "Great Resignation," recently coined by Anthony Klotz, associate professor of management at Mays Business School at \uc2 Texas A&M University\uc2 , posits that when employees are forced back into the toil of a commute and 9-to-5 office job, many will be driven to quit.\par \par \uc2 A global \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  survey showed more than 40% of respondents were considering leaving their employer this year. A widely cited \uc2 Prudential Financial\uc2  survey from March found 1 in 3 U.S. workers wouldn't want to work for an employer that requires them to be on site full time. Prudential Vice Chairman \uc2 Rob Falzon\uc2  said in an interview with Axios that the potential for an ensuing talent war keeps him up at night.\par \par \uc2 The most recent jobs report from the Labor Department showed that four million people quit their jobs in April, taking the quits rate, or quits as a share of employment, to a record 2.7%\par \par \uc2 But luring workers with more flexibility isn't without downside. As president and co-founder of behavioral analytics company Humanyze, Ben Waber has studied team dynamics and work patterns for over a decade. He cautions that companies are taking big swings on remote work based on little to no data. Major effects take many innings to play out.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Waber's research highlights how communication is often sacrificed in a fully remote workforce, and the deficit isn't always perceived. In one survey he ran, the most employees said connectivity improved or stayed the same through remote work, even while he found a 21% reduction in connections outside of an employee's core contacts. Among one of his Fortune 500 clients, he notes communication went up by 20% when employees worked in person just one day a week.\par \par \uc2 Productivity declines also are possible.\par \par \uc2 A paper out of the Becker Friedman Institute for Economics at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  showed productivity fell by about 20% at a large Asian IT services company during the work-from-home period of the pandemic. While total hours worked increased by roughly 30%, average output didn't significantly change, the data showed.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, chief executives from \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  said their employees would need to filter back to the office soon. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's \uc2 James Gorman\uc2  said that while he "isn't dictating yet," his employees should be back by Labor Day to avoid "a different kind of conversation."\par \par \uc2 Many companies such as Apple and Alphabet's \uc2 Google\uc2  are opting for something in between. But hybrid structures have their drawbacks, too. Research from Stanford economics professor \uc2 Nicholas Bloom\uc2  and co-authors Jose Maria Barrero and Steven J. Davis shows how hybrid structures could affect diversity within a company. Among college graduates with children, women want to work from home full time almost 50% more than men, the research shows. Mr. Bloom called that differentiation worrying, given a work-from-home study he ran in 2014 showed a 50% lower rate of promotion after 21 months compared with in-office colleagues.\par \par \uc2 Overall, younger employees appear to be the most restless. The \uc2 Prudential\uc2  survey found 34% of millennials plan to seek a new employer after the pandemic versus 24% of Generation X workers and just 10% of baby boomers.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps they should take a page from \uc2 NYU\uc2  Stern marketing professor \uc2 Scott Galloway\uc2 , who called work-life balance a myth in a blog post earlier this month, noting there are only trade-offs.\par \par \uc2 No boom lasts forever.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich274\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4110
