\par\par \b \uc2 Meatpackers Face Tougher Supervision\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacob Bunge \par \uc2 833 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The American meatpacking industry faces stricter oversight in Washington, as lawmakers and regulators push an overhaul of the $213 billion sector following complaints about meat companies' alleged influence over markets and farmers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Department of Agriculture\uc2  is crafting new rules that would change how companies such as \uc2 Tyson Foods Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Pilgrim's Pride Corp\uc2 . pay chicken farmers, while making it easier for farmers to pursue disputes against meatpackers, the agency said this month. On Capitol Hill, Republican and Democratic lawmakers have proposed legislation that would require beef processors like \uc2 Cargill Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 JBS USA Holdings Inc\uc2 . to buy more cattle on open markets, and set minimum regional prices.\par \par \uc2 Senators this month rolled out a separate bill that would appoint a special investigator to enforce meatpacking regulations and probe potential anticompetitive conduct. The Senate Agriculture Committee is set to hold a hearing Wednesday to examine meatpackers' cattle purchasing -- and how that affects livestock markets and burger prices for consumers.\par \par \uc2 Driving the efforts is some farmers' growing frustration over the scale of the country's biggest meatpackers. Farmer and rancher groups have complained that meat companies have recorded big profits while farmers' incomes have suffered, including through disruptions like 2020's pandemic-driven plant shutdowns and the May cyberattack on JBS. Those challenges followed years of low agricultural commodity prices, which helped push more producers into bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 Andy Green, the USDA's senior adviser for fair and competitive markets, said some of farmers' troubles stem from a handful of companies controlling the bulk of U.S. meat processing.\par \par \uc2 "America's food industry has a monopoly problem," said Mr. Green, a former senior fellow for economic policy at the think tank \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2  who joined the \uc2 USDA\uc2  earlier this year. "There's a lot that we want to do to bring competition back to the market."\par \par \uc2 Nearly three-quarters of the U.S. beef supply is processed by JBS, Tyson, Cargill and \uc2 National Beef Packing Co\uc2 . In pork, \uc2 Smithfield Foods Inc\uc2 ., JBS and Tyson represent about 60% of the market, and in chicken, the five largest players -- including Tyson and JBS subsidiary \uc2 Pilgrim's Pride\uc2  -- handle 60% of the country's wings, breast meat and other poultry products, according to industry estimates.\par \par \uc2 Spokesmen for Tyson, Cargill, JBS and Smithfield declined to comment. A National Beef spokesman didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 North American Meat Institute\uc2 , which represents meat companies, is pushing back against tighter regulations for the industry. \uc2 Mark Dopp\uc2 , the group's head of regulatory affairs, said the \uc2 USDA\uc2 's planned rules would narrow farmers' options to sell livestock, enable frivolous lawsuits and potentially boost supermarket prices for burgers and chicken breasts.\par \par \uc2 The meat industry has faced previous attempted overhauls, Mr. Dopp said. The \uc2 USDA\uc2  under the Obama administration proposed similar rules that were later blocked by Congress and court challenges, he said: "They were a bad idea then, and they're still a bad idea."\par \par \uc2 The big four beef processors' share of the cattle market has held roughly steady for 25 years, according to data compiled by the meat institute, while some cattle producers were earning record profits as recently as 2014. Recent low prices for cattle ranchers showed fundamental market forces at work, Mr. Dopp said, as the disruptions that closed meat plants left a greater supply of cattle on the market, pushing down livestock prices.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Green of the \uc2 USDA\uc2  said those episodes showed the vulnerabilities of concentration, and the need to reduce farmers' and consumers' reliance on a small number of major companies. The \uc2 USDA\uc2  this month said it would provide grants and loans to support new and smaller meat-processing plants as part of a $4 billion program to strengthen the U.S. food system.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, retired chicken farmer Reid Phifer said he had been pushing the \uc2 USDA\uc2  and federal lawmakers for years to strengthen what he said were needed protections for farmers who raise poultry for meatpackers.\par \par \uc2 A so-called tournament system, used by some chicken processors to determine how much farmers are paid for raising chickens, shows the need for reform, he said. Under that system, farmers compete against one another on payment, with the most successful earning more for their chickens, while less productive farmers earn lower pay.\par \par \uc2 "The tournament system's one of the worst things to ever happen to a poultry grower but one of the most ingenious things to ever happen to a poultry company," said Mr. Phifer, who is administrator of Owners/Operators of Commercial Poultry Farms in the USA, a farmer group. Because meat companies supply the chicks and feed, he said, farmers have little control over how much they make.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Green said the \uc2 USDA\uc2  intended to change that system, though he declined to specify how. The \uc2 National Chicken Council\uc2 , which represents poultry companies, said it planned to review the proposals and work with the \uc2 USDA\uc2  and other groups through the rule-making process.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210622eh6m00016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
