\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Infrastructure Funding Remains Its Hurdle\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kristina Peterson and Andrew Duehren \par \uc2 631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 June 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The focus of infrastructure talks shifted Wednesday to a bipartisan group of senators after an initial round of White House discussions broke down, but negotiators face the same hurdles on how to pay for any major new spending package.\par \par \uc2 Discussions intensified among a group of centrist-leaning Senate Republicans and Democrats trying to strike a deal that would spend up to $900 billion over five years, according to people familiar with the talks. A small group of Republicans involved in those discussions huddled Wednesday to hash out details of their proposal, which they discussed with GOP leaders later in the day.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Mitt Romney\uc2  (R., Utah), who is part of the group, said there were eight or nine Republicans involved in developing the new proposal, which he said wouldn't include any tax increases. "That's a red line for Republicans," he said. So far the bipartisan group has declined to specify how it would pay for an infrastructure proposal.\par \par \uc2 The fresh effort came a day after President Biden ended an earlier series of talks with a GOP group led by \uc2 Sen. Shelley Moore Capito\uc2  (R., W.Va.). Senior Republicans were already signaling skepticism that the group would be able to produce a breakthrough that eluded Mr. Biden and Mrs. Capito over weeks of talks.\par \par \uc2 "I wish them every success if they can find the magic key that unlocks the door," \uc2 Sen. John Cornyn\uc2  of Texas, a member of Senate GOP leadership, said Wednesday of the bipartisan group. But, he added, "there's nothing that says that whatever this group agrees to that other Republicans are necessarily going to support -- to me, that's the flaw in this approach."\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer (D., N.Y.) indicated on Tuesday that Democrats were preparing to move forward with at least part of an infrastructure package through a process tied to the budget that would allow legislation to pass with just Democratic support. But some Democrats, most prominently Sen. Joe Manchin (D., W.Va.), have said they don't want to push through legislation on party lines, at least not until all efforts at reaching a bipartisan deal have been exhausted.\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said Wednesday that Mr. Biden believes that there are multiple paths forward and was encouraged by the bipartisan Senate group, as well as a more fleshed-out proposal from the Problem Solvers Caucus, a bipartisan House group. The Problem Solvers on Tuesday night released a more detailed $1.249 trillion proposal over eight years, of which $762 billion would be new spending above a projected baseline of continued federal spending.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Psaki also said the White House was pleased to see the Senate moving forward with the budget process and the ability to pass legislation with Democratic support, known as reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 The negotiations between Mr. Biden and Mrs. Capito faltered in part because the two sides couldn't reach any agreement on how to offset the cost of their proposals, which also remained nearly $700 billion apart in size. Republicans rejected Mr. Biden's call to increase the corporate tax rate, as well as other proposals floated by the White House, which Republicans said amounted to comparable tax increases through different methods.\par \par \uc2 The White House said Republicans weren't willing to spend enough to meet Mr. Biden's threshold for a sufficiently sweeping infrastructure, clean energy and job creation package.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden also faces pressure from his party's left wing, which raised concerns Wednesday over the prospect of omitting some of the more ambitious proposals to combat climate change.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers face a Sept. 30 deadline for reauthorizing many federal infrastructure programs, a step Congress must take every few years.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210610eh6a0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
