\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Teen Summer Job Market Sizzles --- Employers turn to young people to fill gaps as U.S. emerges from long lockdown\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick Thomas \par \uc2 804 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Young Americans are saving the summer.\par \par \uc2 Businesses are counting on teenage workers to staff restaurants, golf clubs, resorts and other hot-weather entertainment spots emerging from pandemic lockdowns. Many employers are struggling to find enough adult workers, and so to fill the gap they are leaning on teens like never before and heavily courting them to keep businesses running in a busy summer.\par \par \uc2 For many young adults now flooding into the hot summer labor market, conditions are creating a job bonanza, complete with more accommodating bosses, greater schedule flexibility and even higher pay than in summers past.\par \par \uc2 Teens are answering the call to work. In May, the share of 16- to 19-year-olds who work rose to 33.2%, the highest since 2008, according to figures released by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  on Friday. That teen-employment rate is still far off the near-50% levels of the 1970s, when summer and part-time jobs were more common rites of passage. But it marks a sharp rebound from the record-low 20% employment rate among teens in April 2020, shortly after the pandemic set in and dried up millions of lower-wage job opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Likewise, the unemployment rate for 16- to 19-year-olds dropped in May to 9.6% from 12.3% in April, its lowest level since 1953, according to the federal jobs data.\par \par \uc2 Ric Serrano, chief executive of Serrano's Mexican Restaurants, with five locations in the Phoenix area, says he is turning to teens to solve a hiring crunch.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Serrano had to close his restaurants on Mondays temporarily because he couldn't hire enough workers; they are already closed on Sundays. Now he is offering a $200 bonus to employees who stay on for at least four months and is hoping younger workers can plug holes in his staffing.\par \par \uc2 "Maybe it is all the kids getting out of school that will change this," he says. "For kids coming out of school who are looking to make some dough, it's a perfect storm for them."\par \par \uc2 Many of the businesses now facing labor shortages are big employers of teenagers to begin with. At the end of March, there were 1.2 million open jobs in the leisure and hospitality sectors and 734,000 open retail jobs. Last year, about 40% of working teens worked in leisure and hospitality, while retailers employed another 25%.\par \par \uc2 Grace Vondra, a 17-year-old from Hillsdale, Mich., plans to spend the summer waiting tables at Rosalie's Roadhouse in nearby Jonesville, Mich. Ms. Vondra, who graduates from high school this month, says finding a job with a flexible schedule was key for her and her classmates.\par \par \uc2 "There's definitely a lot more jobs open," Ms. Vondra says. "All my friends have jobs. And it's not really typical for all of them to have jobs."\par \par \uc2 Luke Pardue, an economist for Gusto, a payroll and benefits company with more than 100,000 small-business clients, says teens are often more willing to work for lower wages than older workers.\par \par \uc2 "We've seen a pent-up demand for tourism and recreation jobs where teens are most likely to be employed," Mr. Pardue says. "Hesitancy among older workers, a higher demand for these types of jobs, could combine to make 2021 a record year for teen employment."\par \par \uc2 Itai Ben Eli, who runs Doris Metropolitan steakhouses in Houston and New Orleans, was planning to open a new bakery in Houston called Badolina in May this year. He had to delay it until June because he couldn't find enough workers. Out of 45 scheduled interviews, two showed up, he said.\par \par \uc2 Over the past couple of weeks, Mr. Ben Eli says he has gotten an influx of new resumes, and more applicants are showing up to interview -- all of them teenagers. So far, he has hired eight to help launch the bakery.\par \par \uc2 "They are 100% of my staffing right now," says Mr. Ben Eli. He is hoping his staffing shortages resolve themselves by the fall, when many teens return to school. "I've never seen anything like this," he says.\par \par \uc2 Brooke Porter, 20, was furloughed from her $11-an-hour retail job at an \uc2 Ulta Beauty Inc\uc2 . store in Omaha, Neb., last year. Now, the store is asking her to come back for $15 an hour, she says. Ms. Porter says she is considering it, but also plans to apply at a Sephora store to see which location gives her the better offer.\par \par \uc2 "They are trying to get people in there fast," she says. "If you just show a sign that you are interested, they are going to talk to you further."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich362\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal5430
