\par\par \b \uc2 Major Work Complete on New LIRR Hub\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger \par \uc2 554 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A9A\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A long-delayed, $11 billion project to bring \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  trains into Manhattan's East Side is almost complete, New York Gov. Andrew Cuomo said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 As the governor walked the eight-block long concourse of the new station built below Grand Central Terminal he said major construction on tracks, platforms and the 350,000 square-foot station is finished.\par \par \uc2 "This will make a major difference for Long Islanders," Mr. Cuomo said.\par \par \uc2 The station is expected to open by the end of next year.\par \par \uc2 Electrical and other work remains. So, too, does a monthslong process to teach drivers how to navigate the eight new miles of track between Queens and Manhattan, which includes crossing over some of the busiest rail lines in the U.S. The training is set to begin this summer.\par \par \uc2 The station will expand commuting options on the East Side for tens of thousands of LIRR riders who use New York Penn Station at 34th Street between Seventh and Eighth avenues on Manhattan's west side.\par \par \uc2 Transportation officials expect 80,000 LIRR commuters to use the new connection, known as East Side Access.\par \par \uc2 Those projections were made before the pandemic forced millions of people in the region to work from home. Weekday LIRR ridership is about 65% below pre-pandemic norms, agency data show. Some transit agencies believe many suburban commuters will continue to work from home after the pandemic is over, at least a few days a week.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuomo said he is confident that riders will return if the LIRR, which is run by the state-controlled \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , delivers good service and amenities at the new station.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 MTA\uc2 's construction and development officer, \uc2 Janno Lieber\uc2 , who walked the station with Mr. Cuomo, pointed out how the long concourse would allow commuters to spread out and relieve congestion. The concourse has multiple entrances and 17 escalators descending 140 feet to a mezzanine with access to eight tracks that can serve up to 24 trains an hour.\par \par \uc2 Two new buildings in the area, \uc2 SL Green Realty Corp\uc2 .'s One Vanderbilt and \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2 .'s 270 Park Ave, will have direct access to the subterranean station.\par \par \uc2 At platform level Mr. Cuomo indicated that wall tiles and tracks have been engineered to absorb noise and make the place more pleasant.\par \par \uc2 Construction on the new station began about 15 years ago. The project was supposed to be completed in 2013. But the deadline was pushed back several times because of delays caused by construction challenges and the difficulty of coordinating work among dozens of contractors and three rail lines: the LIRR, \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  and NJ Transit.\par \par \uc2 Challenges included tunneling in loose soil beneath major city roads and elevated subway line footings, as well as rebuilding one of the nation's busiest passenger-rail interchanges, used by more than 600 trains a day.\par \par \uc2 In 2009, the project's cost was estimated at $7.3 billion. The most recent estimate, in 2018, put the cost at more than $11 billion.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuomo described the project as dormant when he took office in 2011. He said it has been the most difficult infrastructure project of his governorship. "We inherited a pit, literally," he said. "And we had to dig our way out."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210528eh5s0001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
