\par\par \b \uc2 Labor Groups Split Over Plan to Let App Workers Unionize\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jimmy Vielkind \par \uc2 609 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 May 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A13A\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ALBANY -- Worker groups are divided over a proposal in New York state that would let ride-hail drivers and delivery workers form unions and get access to additional benefits, while sidestepping the question of whether they should be classified as employees.\par \par \uc2 State \uc2 Sen. Diane Savino\uc2 , a Democrat from Staten Island, began circulating a draft of the proposed legislation last week. She said she was having ongoing discussions with labor groups and app companies -- including \uc2 DoorDash Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Lyft Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Uber Technologies Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Savino hopes her bill will be enacted before the state Legislature adjourns for summer recess on June 10. But groups representing delivery workers, as well as labor unions who voiced support for the measure backed away this week, reducing the odds of rapid action, lawmakers said.\par \par \uc2 Representatives for Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Andrea Stewart-Cousins\uc2  and Assembly Speaker \uc2 Carl Heastie\uc2 , both Democrats, didn't respond to requests for comment. A spokesman for \uc2 Gov. Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , a Democrat, said: "This is an issue that we've been looking at and we will review this and any other similar proposals."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Savino estimated her bill would affect more than 200,000 people. Most app-based workers are considered independent contractors, and as such aren't covered by New York's wage and hour laws, or by state systems of workers' compensation and unemployment insurance.\par \par \uc2 According to a bill draft reviewed by The Wall Street Journal, workers in both the delivery and ride-hail sector would be allowed to organize under a labor-peace process overseen by a state board. Companies would provide contact information for workers, and a union would be certified if it collected statements of interest from 10% of workers in a sector -- a lower threshold than under federal law, said labor leaders.\par \par \uc2 Organizers would agree not to disparage companies or disrupt their operations with actions such as strikes. Bargaining would then occur through an industry council between a union and companies that operate in the sector, the plan says.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of the proposal say it would lead to immediate benefits for thousands of workers, including a workers' compensation system, while giving them a chance to bargain collectively. Opponents contend it could undermine a larger effort by organized labor to win legal employee status for workers in a growing sector.\par \par \uc2 California lawmakers passed a law in 2019 that classified many app-based workers as employees rather than independent contractors. After a $200 million campaign funded by Uber, \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  and others, Californians voted to exempt the companies in a ballot measure last year.\par \par \uc2 Unions in New York had sought to emulate the California legislation. App companies pushed back, saying their workers valued flexibility that was incompatible with a traditional employee classification. The companies have instead advocated for portable benefits arrangements. Ride-hail drivers can access workers' compensation benefits through a private system administered by the nonprofit Black Car Fund.\par \par \uc2 The companies have engaged with Ms. Savino and didn't oppose the early drafts of the legislation, people familiar with the matter said. Representatives for \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment, and an Uber spokeswoman declined to comment. A Grubhub spokesman said the company looked forward to reviewing the plan.\par \par \uc2 The Independent Drivers Guild, an affiliate of the Machinists Union that represents ride-hail drivers and receives some funding from Uber, supports the bill. New York Taxi Workers Alliance Executive Director Bhairavi Desai said the bargaining process under the bill would pre-empt a minimum pay for ride-hail drivers guaranteed by the New York City Taxi & Limousine Commission, a regulator.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210527eh5r0001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
