\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Careers & Leadership: Gen Z Turns to TikTok For Tips on Finding Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kathryn Dill \par \uc2 823 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 May 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jackie Cuevas has read a lot of lackluster emails from job seekers. One particularly uninspired entry last May prompted the 30-year-old human-resources manager to take to TikTok.\par \par \uc2 In a 55-second video posted to her account, she explained why the phrase, "Hello, I was wondering if you guys were looking to hire," isn't the way to grab a potential employer's attention. Her video garnered 11,000 views -- a lot at the time for Ms. Cuevas, whose previous posts mostly featured her cat or her lip-syncing talents.\par \par \uc2 She now posts career advice multiple times a week as "Jackie, your friend in HR," fielding questions from nearly 150,000 followers on everything from phone-interview tips to getting a resume through an applicant-tracking system.\par \par \uc2 "Of course there's a career center and counselors, but upon getting closer to the real world, they're like, 'I have no idea what I want to do or need to do,'" says Ms. Cuevas of her predominantly 20-something TikTok audience.\par \par \uc2 To the casual observer, TikTok is an app known for animal videos, choreographed dances or those weeks in January when everyone was inexplicably performing sea shanties. Click on #HRTikTok, #careeradvice or even #recruitersbelike, and career advice -- from "How to quit like a pro" to "3 things you never wanna say in an interview" to "Stop saying I'm sorry in the workplace!!" -- awaits.\par \par \uc2 Guidance ranges from practical ("How to answer 'What's your biggest weakness?'") to aspirational ("How to find your dream career"), delivered in under a minute and accompanied by colorful on-screen text, audio clips and occasional dance moves.\par \par \uc2 Much of the advice is geared toward the older members of Gen Z -- the generation born roughly between 1997 and 2015 and the one most closely associated with the platform. Many of those delivering the straight-to-camera wisdom are 20- or early-30-somethings themselves who say they are bridging the staid world of career counseling and an environment where anxious early-career job seekers feel more at ease.\par \par \uc2 Some videos tackle the anxieties of being an entry-level worker, encouraging users to stop over-apologizing or using nervous filler phrases such as "like" and "you know."\par \par \uc2 Mia Williams, 24, is a senior analyst at Accenture PLC and the founder of The Colors of Her Success, a career-resource site for women of color in their 20s. She started posting career content on TikTok last month, drawing on her time so far in the corporate world.\par \par \uc2 As a student working in the campus career office at \uc2 Towson University\uc2  in Maryland, Ms. Williams said she noticed that college students tended to undersell themselves. In her most popular video -- shot at her parents' suburban house in Bowie, Md., where she currently lives -- she riffs on how TikTokkers should talk to potential employers about retail jobs they have had: "Did you 'assist customers?' Or did you 'improve the customer's shopping experience by utilizing product knowledge to provide personalized recommendations that led to increased sales revenue?'"\par \par \uc2 Career coaches on TikTok empathize with the daunting task of finding a toehold in the professional world. Alexa Shoen, 32, has built a coaching business influenced by her own early-career confusion for panicking entry-level job seekers. She started posting to the platform in January after watching college students and recent graduates on TikTok commiserate over the sadness of the previous year. A former \uc2 Facebook\uc2  employee, Ms. Shoen was hired by TikTok in May for a product-marketing role.\par \par \uc2 Much of the advice on TikTok echoes the general tips to be found in career-advice books, podcasts and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  videos. It is just the aesthetics and form that are different.\par \par \uc2 "People are able to get access to what they would have in a coaching session that would have cost hundreds of dollars, even a thousand dollars, an hour," says David Paykin, 25, a coach who launched his TikTok account in October and now has 1.5 million followers.\par \par \uc2 Career coach Madeline Mann, 29, says that at a previous HR job, she would often reply to job applicants with suggestions for improving their resumes or cover letters. "They wrote back saying, 'Get a life,'" she says.\par \par \uc2 When she started posting the same advice on TikTok in 2019 with colorful text on-screen and clever editing, she got a flood of questions like, "what kind of jobs can I get with this degree?" that she says clearly came from a younger audience.\par \par \uc2 She has recently seen more activity from millennials, Gen Xers and baby boomers on the platform. "The questions I get more now are, 'I'm interviewing for this company, how should I answer this question about why I left my last job?' or "How do I follow up with this company I haven't heard from?'" she says.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210524eh5o0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
