\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Moves to Curb Injuries --- Online retailer is rolling out safety videos, stretching exercises and mediation zones\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 505 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . is establishing a program focused on improving the health and wellness of its hourly warehouse staffers, after years of criticism over worker safety at its depots and a pledge by Chief Executive \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  to offer a better vision for employees.\par \par \uc2 The company said Monday that its new program, called WorkingWell, aims to better educate some employees on how to avoid workplace injuries and improve mental health on the job.\par \par \uc2 The online retailer began testing parts of the program two years ago and plans to expand it to 1,000 facilities by the end of the year, said Heather MacDougall, vice president of world-wide workplace health and safety. The company said it aims to cut recordable incidents in half by 2025. The program has been in 350 sites in North America and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  recorded 5.6 injuries per 100 workers in 2019, the last full year of data, compared with the 4.8 rate nationally for the warehousing and storage sector, according to company and federal workplace data. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  says it monitors injuries more closely than other companies, which might cause its reported injury rate to be higher.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bezos said last month that the retailer needed to do a better job for employees after a recent, unsuccessful push by some Alabama warehouse workers to unionize. The vote is under appeal. In a letter to shareholders, he called reports of unsafe working conditions at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouses inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 Ms. MacDougall said \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , which employs about 950,000 people in the U.S., was acting because of the frequency of workplace injuries in the warehousing industry and because the pandemic heightened the awareness of healthcare needs. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is particularly concerned about musculoskeletal disorders, she said. The disorders, known as MSDs, account for 40% of work-related injuries across the retailer's facilities, it said.\par \par \uc2 "We've recognized that there is work to be done when it comes to reducing MSDs," Ms. MacDougall said.\par \par \uc2 Under the WorkingWell program, warehouse employees gather on a rotating basis near their work stations to watch videos about injury prevention, including how to lift items properly. Depending on their roles, employees are given hourly prompts at their stations that guide them through stretching and breathing exercises. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said the exercise breaks can last from 30 seconds to a minute each.\par \par \uc2 The company is installing kiosks where employees can watch videos that show guided meditations and calming scenes and sounds. New wellness zones provide dedicated spaces for workers to stretch or meditate, and the company plans to introduce a mobile app that lets employees view similar tools at home. The company is developing staffing schedules that rotate employees among jobs that use different muscle groups to reduce repetitive-stress injuries.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's program doesn't include a significant reduction in the rate at which employees are expected to work. That pace has been a source of worker complaints.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210518eh5i0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
