\par\par \b \uc2 Business News -- Future of Everything: CEO Ties Enthusiasm to Office --- WeWork boss says those most engaged on job are also the ones who want to return\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kathryn Dill \par \uc2 556 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The CEO of WeWork thinks there is an easy way for companies to spot their most engaged employees: They're the ones who want to come back to the office.\par \par \uc2 "Those who are uberly engaged with the company want to go to the office two-thirds of the time, at least," \uc2 Sandeep Mathrani\uc2  said Wednesday at The Wall Street Journal's Future of Everything Festival. "Those who are least engaged are very comfortable working from home."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mathrani said that while many employees were hoping for a hybrid future after the pandemic that would afford them to work from home a few days a week, time at the office was essential for collaboration and efficiency.\par \par \uc2 "People are happier when they come to work," said Mr. Mathrani, a veteran of the commercial real-estate industry. "The bigger issue is do you come to work five days a week or do you come to work three days a week? That's the bigger issue. There's no issue of not coming to a common place."\par \par \uc2 Coming to the office at least part-time would allow workers to reinstate boundaries between work and home life that may have eroded during the pandemic and to combat Zoom fatigue, Mr. Mathrani said. Other top executives, including JP Morgan Chase CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , have recently noted their weariness with convening via the platform. Even Zoom CEO \uc2 Eric Yuan\uc2  said he had experienced Zoom fatigue.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mathrani took the reins at WeWork in February 2020, just weeks before lockdown orders forced offices to close. Pandemic-driven furloughs and layoffs across industries provided cover for the beleaguered company, he said, as WeWork let go of roughly 3,000 people, on top of thousands more workers WeWork shed the previous year.\par \par \uc2 After one of the most spectacular IPO implosions in recent years -- the IPO never happened -- WeWork announced in March plans to go public via merger with a SPAC called \uc2 BowX Acquisition Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Despite the company's tumult, Mr. Mathrani said other companies' post-pandemic offices might look a lot like a WeWork site -- not just an open-plan office, but a beautiful one, free of clutter and designed to promote collaboration by allowing employees to sign up for desks and conference rooms.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of the things this company did pre-pandemic is really what companies want to do post-pandemic," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mathrani said he still spoke to former WeWork CEO and co-founder \uc2 Adam Neumann\uc2  every few weeks and was quick to note his achievements. "It's easy to look at the things that went wrong," he said of Mr. Neumann's leadership of the company. "But there were a lot of things that went right when he created a brand that's synonymous with flexibility."\par \par \uc2 Post-pandemic, Mr. Mathrani forecasts a WeWork known more for providing flexibility than fun. When asked if WeWork's own offices would be bringing back the beer on tap and tequila shots, he was resolute.\par \par \uc2 "Fun is not defined by those aspects," said Mr. Mathrani. "I'm 59 years old. I'm uber serious, and so maybe having an adult in the room will change that."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210513eh5d0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
