\par\par \b \uc2 N.J. Lawmakers Threaten to Strike Back\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph De Avila and Paul Berger \par \uc2 474 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10A\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A group of New Jersey lawmakers is threatening to retaliate if New York moves forward with its plan to charge drivers a fee to enter Manhattan's central business district.\par \par \uc2 State Sen. Joseph Lagana and Assemblyman Christopher Tully, both Democrats, said they are crafting legislation that would require nonresidents to pay state sales tax on tolls when driving between New Jersey and New York at crossings such as the George Washington Bridge.\par \par \uc2 The tax revenue would be used to reimburse New Jersey drivers who have to pay so-called congestion-pricing fees to travel into Manhattan, under New York's new program.\par \par \uc2 The congestion-pricing program, which was established by New York state in 2019, was supposed to be implemented earlier this year. But an environmental review by the \uc2 Federal Highway Administration\uc2  was held up under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration resumed the review process at the end of March. The review is expected to be completed later this year, paving the way for final design and construction of the tolling infrastructure. The \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , which is controlled by New York state, has said charges may begin in early 2023.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lagana said New York's congestion-pricing plan, if approved, would unfairly punish commuters from his state.\par \par \uc2 "New Jersey is not New York City's piggy bank," Mr. Lagana said. "If they move forward with this scheme, it would be irresponsible not to find a way to protect our residents."\par \par \uc2 U.S. Rep. Josh Gottheimer (D., N.J.) said he plans to write to the Transportation Department to urge the Biden administration to reject New York's plan. He said he supports the efforts of state lawmakers in New Jersey to strike back with a sales tax on out-of-state drivers.\par \par \uc2 New York's congestion charge would apply to vehicles entering a zone covering most of Manhattan between 60th Street and Battery Park. Those charges are expected to apply once each day to most vehicles entering the congestion zone. They will be set by a six-member panel chosen by the \uc2 MTA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The panel will have the power to grant credits, discounts or exemptions to certain groups, including those who have paid tolls on crossings. But it would be bound by the program's goal to reduce congestion, while raising about $1 billion in annual revenues to pay for mass-transit upgrades in New York.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tully said New Jersey drivers shouldn't be expected to fix the financial problems of the subway system.\par \par \uc2 Ken Lovett, an MTA spokesman said: "Congestion pricing will benefit everyone who comes to New York, including those from New Jersey, by providing reduced traffic and better air quality."\par \par \uc2 Gov. Andrew Cuomo\uc2 's office didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210511eh5b00017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
