\par\par \b \uc2 Banking & Finance: \uc2 MetLife\uc2 , \uc2 Allstate\uc2  Please Analysts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leslie Scism \par \uc2 717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MetLife Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Allstate Corp\uc2 . posted strong gains in the adjusted earnings watched by analysts and investors.\par \par \uc2 The Covid-19 pandemic fueled another strong quarter for \uc2 Allstate\uc2 's car-insurance business with rush-hour fender benders still running below normal due to so many Americans working from home. \uc2 MetLife\uc2  had both positive and negative benefits from Covid-19: sharply higher death benefits on life-insurance policies but reduced payouts on pension products and annuities.\par \par \uc2 "We believe the worst impact of the pandemic on our business performance is behind us," MetLife Chief Executive \uc2 Michel Khalaf\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic's impact on the insurance industry hasn't been as harmful as feared when Covid-19 cases started climbing just over a year ago. Car insurers got windfall profits as stay-at-home directives and business shutdowns left vehicles parked for days. While death claims are elevated, U.S. life insurers have paid out fewer claims than initially expected per 100,000 Covid-19 fatalities, largely because the virus has disproportionately killed the elderly and other people with little to no insurance.\par \par \uc2 MetLife\uc2 's net income fell 93% to $290 million, a decline primarily related to mark-to-market losses on the financial hedges it buys to protect the company from falling interest rates and stock markets. When rates and equities rise, as they did in the first quarter, the value of that protection falls. Still, \uc2 MetLife\uc2 's reported results got a lift from the improved market conditions.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street analysts largely look past those swings and focus on "adjusted earnings," which strip out items considered nonrecurring. On that basis, \uc2 MetLife\uc2  reported a 36% surge to $1.97 billion. Much of the improvement came from unusually strong returns from private-equity holdings in the company's huge investment portfolio. It has a tiny percentage of such holdings but they helped fuel a 74% gain from the year-earlier period to $5.31 billion in net investment income.\par \par \uc2 Allstate\uc2  posted a net loss of $1.41 billion, a reversal from net income of $513 million in the year-earlier period. The results reflected an aggregate net loss of about $4 billion in the quarter from previously announced agreements to sell a pair of life- and annuity insurers.\par \par \uc2 Allstate\uc2 's adjusted net income rose 56% to $1.87 billion. The improvement came even as catastrophe losses more than doubled, to $590 million from $211 million.\par \par \uc2 A big part of the improvement came from the auto-insurance business, thanks to "decreased accident frequency and a lower expense ratio," the company said.\par \par \uc2 Miles driven across the U.S. "is headed up to pre-pandemic levels, but not always at the same time of day" as people used to drive, Allstate Chief Executive \uc2 Tom Wilson\uc2  said. "It looks like we are coming out of the reduction in auto accidents slowly, but nobody knows where it is going" as Americans return to their workplaces. "You could surmise that as the country gets back to work, it will go back up."\par \par \uc2 Allstate\uc2 , which also is one of the nation's biggest home insurers, was among property insurers hit with large claims volume from the long stretch of frigid February weather in Texas and numerous other states. The unusual weather across Southern states caused widespread freezing and burst pipes, leading to extensive water damage.\par \par \uc2 Industrywide, property insurers face an estimated $18 billion bill for the cold-weather damage to homes and businesses, the equivalent cost of a major hurricane, according to a risk-modeling firm, Karen Clark & Co.\par \par \uc2 Like its rival \uc2 Prudential Financial Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 MetLife\uc2  experienced higher costs in this year's first quarter, compared with the year before, in its business of selling life insurance to employers' benefits programs.\par \par \uc2 Both \uc2 Prudential\uc2  and \uc2 MetLife\uc2  also have large retirement-income businesses that have benefited as pensioners and owners of annuities have died prematurely, the companies' results show.\par \par \uc2 Prudential\uc2  reported its first-quarter results Tuesday. It said its group-insurance business lost $132 million in the first quarter on an adjusted basis, compared with operating profit of $44 million in the year-earlier period, a decline largely driven by Covid-19 deaths. Its retirement-income business reported adjusted profit of $623 million, up from $245 million, "including favorable impacts due to Covid-19."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210506eh560003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
