\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 WSJ CEO Council: Even Zoom's CEO Says He Has Zoom Fatigue\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter \par \uc2 463 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some of America's top executives, even including the CEO of Zoom Video Communications Inc., say they are souring on some aspects of remote work.\par \par \uc2 After more than a year of working virtually during the pandemic, executives in banking and technology are pushing back on the idea that workers should be able to do their jobs entirely from home in the coming months. Though some said they expect more flexible work arrangements to endure going forward, they say there are clear signs of burnout in an era of nonstop video calls.\par \par \uc2 Eric Yuan\uc2 , chief executive of Zoom, told a virtual audience of The Wall Street Journal's CEO Council Summit on Tuesday that he personally experienced Zoom fatigue. On one day last year, he said he had 19 Zoom meetings in a row. "I'm so tired of that," Mr. Yuan said, adding that he no longer books back-to-back Zoom calls. "I do have meeting fatigue."\par \par \uc2 Like many companies, Zoom is planning an eventual return to its offices, he said. Zoom's employees will most likely be asked to come into an office two days a week, he said.\par \par \uc2 For months, corporate leaders have debated how best to reorient work once more employees return to in-person settings. Though many are settling on a hybrid approach, blending remote work with in-office days, there is a standoff between some workers and bosses around how flexible work should be after the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Some employees have gotten so used to remote work that they never want to go back to an office, said \uc2 Ellen Kullman\uc2 , CEO of 3-D printing startup Carbon Inc. While employees should have some input, they alone won't decide how work evolves, she added.\par \par \uc2 "I might be old school, but I'm a believer that over time it will migrate back to a more in-person environment," Ms. Kullman said.\par \par \uc2 Plenty of bosses want to adjust their approach to work. Richard Moore, chief of the U.K. Secret Intelligence Service, also known as MI6, said at the CEO summit that he was urging his organization not to "snap back to old ways of doing things" post-pandemic.\par \par \uc2 "It is not the same but you can fake it pretty well," he said of people's ability to connect through their computer screens. Though he personally doesn't want to work from home, he said, "we are trying to be better about flexible working."\par \par \uc2 Nasdaq Inc\uc2 . Chief Executive \uc2 Adena Friedman\uc2  said that, in the coming months, employers in urban areas such as New York may need to "encourage and entice employees to come back into the city," including more flexible work and commuting arrangements.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020210505eh550001e\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0